//! core/proc — ending the process.
//!
//! One function, because `Proc` declares one operation. It is a module of its
//! own rather than a line in `core/io` for the reason the effects are split at
//! all: exiting is a different authority from printing, and a reader of a
//! context can see which of them a program was given.
//!
//! `exit` and not `exitWith`, which reads as a method — the name was written
//! for `ctx.exitWith(3)`, and the call is now `proc.exit(ctx, 3)`, which is the
//! sentence.
//!
//! Granted on LINUX, MACOS and JS, and withheld from WEB: a page has no process
//! to exit, and a mounted interface stays live.

from "core/effect" import { Proc };

/// Ends the process with `code`, immediately.
///
/// It does not return, and there is no way to say so in a signature — a
/// function whose type says `()` and never produces one is the honest shape
/// available. Nothing after the call runs.
///
/// **A `main` that ends normally should return rather than call this.**
/// `.Ok(())` exits 0 and `.Err(msg)` prints `msg` and exits 1, which is the
/// whole of the ordinary vocabulary; this is for the program that has to name
/// a *particular* status — a tool whose exit code is part of its interface.
export fn exit<C: Proc>(ctx: C, code: Int): () {
    ctx.exitWith(code)
}
